<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transylvania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Transylvania is a historical region in the Carpathian basin, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. It was an autonomous principality under Ottoman suzerainty in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, intervening in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">Deluge</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> of 1699 transferred it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, and it later formed part of the largely rural economy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transylvania.jpg" alt="Transylvania" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BlinxTheKitty · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Transylvania_(Local_2-3).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…I. The title gave a common name to the composite monarchy the Habsburgs had built since the sixteenth century: the Austrian hereditary lands, the Lands of the Bohemian Crown, the Kingdom of Hungary with Croatia, the Grand Principality of <b>Transylvania</b>, and Galicia, taken in the partitions of Poland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…the region one of the most productive industrial zones in continental Europe; the commercial Vienna basin with its banking, insurance and professional services; the plains of Hungary, among the great grain-producing regions of Europe; and the predominantly rural economies of Galicia, Bukovina, Dalmatia, <b>Transylvania</b> and, after 1878, Bosnia-Herzegovina.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Khmelnytsky's multi-vectored diplomacy, weighing an Orthodox–Protestant bloc, a third partner's place in the Commonwealth or the Ottoman orbit, drew the Tsardom, Poland, Sweden, <b>Transylvania</b> and the Porte into the Ukrainian question.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ottoman Sultan Suleiman the Magnificent backed Zápolya, besieged Vienna in 1529 and occupied Buda in 1541, leaving Hungary divided between a Habsburg ‘Royal Hungary’ in the north and west, an Ottoman province in the centre and an autonomous Principality of <b>Transylvania</b> in the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Stephen then extended his rule over the semi-independent chiefs of the basin: he overthrew his maternal uncle Gyula in <b>Transylvania</b> around 1003 and later, probably in the 1020s, destroyed Ahtum, who ruled the lands along the Maros.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the same time much of the country was controlled by great lords, the so-called oligarchs, who ruled their regions as independent princes: Matthew Csák in the north-west, Amadeus Aba in the north-east, Ladislaus Kán in <b>Transylvania</b> and the Kőszegi family in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
